{"page": 8, "kind": "cell", "table": 1, "row": 9, "col": 3, "x": null, "text": "Content Standard SS.5.6.19.1 Analyze how economic and political conflicts between the colonies and England led to the American Revolution", "id": "u188", "furniture": false}
{"page": 8, "kind": "cell", "table": 1, "row": 9, "col": 4, "x": null, "text": "• Economic: colonial prosperity, efforts to regulate trade with Stamp and Townshend Acts, growth of commerce and markets, trade and the Atlantic economy • Political: Stamp Act Congress, Coercive Acts, Continental Congress, committees of correspondents, non-importation movements, newspapers and communication, Paine and Common Sense, growth of a sense of British identity, demands for the “Rights of Englishmen” and more colonial political authority, Republican ideas about virtue, power, and corruption • Conflicts: Seven Years War, Boston Tea Party, Boston Massacre, Sons of Liberty, Battle of Bunker Hill, Lexington and Concord", "id": "u189", "furniture": false}
{"page": 8, "kind": "cell", "table": 1, "row": 10, "col": 1, "x": null, "text": "Anchor Standard 19", "id": "u190", "furniture": false}
